# Surface: the terminal — tmux, and the few keys WezTerm still owns.
# Schema, verify kinds and writing rules: see README.md in this directory.
#
# Every entry here is host-only: a shell inside a container cannot press these.
# `mode: "terminal"` is what marks that.
#
# tmux stays on THIS surface rather than getting a fifth `--mode`: from the
# user's seat it *is* the terminal. tmux-key targets resolve against
# `tmux -L <label> -f <conf> list-keys`; they name `key`, `mods` and an
# optional `table` (`root` when absent).
#
# WHAT `mods` MEANS HERE. A tmux binding
# spells its modifiers in the key itself — `C-S-x`, not a separate field — so
# a tmux-key target carries `mods: "NONE"` and puts the whole chord in `key`,
# exactly as `list-keys` prints it. The WezTerm trap the old header warned
# about at length (a control+shift binding on a letter printed as the
# UPPERCASE letter with `mods = 'CTRL'`) still applies to the three
# `wezterm-key` targets left in this file, and to nothing else.
[
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- terminal
    {
        key: "F5"
        title: "Open the cockpit; an arrow in it moves between panes"
        use: "Press `F5` in any pane — a shell, the editor, an agent — and the cockpit opens. It is the only function key: every action lives under it by mnemonic, `k ➜ label` in columns with the key's letter lit, a `+` marking a group. Top level: `f` Yazi file manager, `s` search, `g` git (`g l` is lazygit), `w` window, `p` pane, `t` theme, `m` modify, `l` lock, `d` detach, `r` reload the tmux config; `Backspace` goes up, `Escape` closes. Press an arrow in it and the cockpit closes into move mode, already one pane that way; Alt and an arrow splits the whole window that way at once (the new pane runs the full height or width), Shift and an arrow splits only the pane you are in, both focus the new pane and return to normal mode, ready to type. Press a digit `1`–`9` and you go to that window of the session; a missing one is made at `~`, so `F5 3` always lands on window 3. `F5 s s` finds a pane by name or content and swaps it in."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 0
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s s", "F5 <arrow>"]
        why: "The menu is the thing to reach for first, and moving is the next most common thing, so an arrow is the way out of it into moving. Native tmux bindings move and arm the `jump` table on the client, so move mode's keys are plain key tables that act the instant they are pressed."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 f"
        title: "Browse files with Yazi in the overlay"
        use: "Press `F5` then `f` to open Yazi in the active pane's working directory. Navigate with arrows or `hjkl`; `Enter` opens a text file in Neovim inside the same overlay. Quit Neovim to return to Yazi, then press `q` or `Esc` when idle to close the overlay and return to the original pane. `Esc` first cancels selections, searches, filters, or pending cut/copy state; dialogs and Neovim keep their own Escape behavior, and unfinished tasks retain Yazi's quit warning."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "files"
        step: 0
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5", "y"]
        why: "Yazi's blocking Neovim opener keeps editing in the overlay rather than creating a separate tmux pane."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 l"
        title: "Lock the terminal behind your login password"
        use: "Press `F5` then `l`. The screen clears to the wallpaper as it really is — no blur, no darkening, no theme tint — with one empty rounded box in the centre; what you type fills it from the middle outwards as dots. Type your macOS login password and press `Enter`; nothing else gets through: tmux ignores every key, `Ctrl+C`, `Ctrl+Z` and `Ctrl+D` do nothing, a wrong password says so and waits a second. Closing the WezTerm window does not help either: the next client that attaches is locked the same way. On the right password the session comes back exactly as it was, wallpaper included."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 0
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5", "F5 d"]
        why: "tmux's own `lock-server` runs the lock in the client's real terminal and stops reading keys until it exits, so no key table or popup can be reached around it; the password is checked against the system account (`dscl . -authonly`), so there is no second secret to keep."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 s s"
        title: "Search sessions: find a pane by name or content, swap it in"
        use: "Press `F5 s s` and every pane is listed, one row each: its name (title or program), what runs where (program, directory, session) and when you last used it, the most recent first and the pane you are in last. The preview is the pane's screen. Type anything and the list narrows to the panes whose name or last 5000 lines contain it, in any case. `Enter` swaps the chosen pane into the one you are in; the pane that was there takes its place. `Ctrl-D` closes the highlighted pane, whatever runs in it, except the one you came from. `Ctrl-X` closes every listed pane that sits at a bare shell prompt, except the one you came from — type first to narrow what it closes. `Tab` switches to the backlog: only the panes you have not touched for an hour or more (or never), and `Tab` again switches back; `Ctrl-X` in the backlog closes only its idle shells. `Escape` closes the picker."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 1
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5", "F5 <arrow>"]
        why: "A pane is a process to reach like an editor buffer, so there is no address to remember: you find it by what it says. The picker is fzf rather than television because television filters only the text it shows and cannot re-run its source per keystroke; every keystroke re-runs `rg` over one capture per pane taken when the picker opened. Last used is the time of the input that focused the pane, stamped by a `pane-focus-in` hook into the pane's `@used` option, which travels with the pane through a swap. `Enter` is `swap-pane`, so nothing is killed, and a hidden session such as `_park` works as a shelf."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 w w"
        title: "Replace this pane with a fresh shell"
        use: "Press `F5 w w` and a new shell takes this pane's place, in the same directory. What was running here is not closed: it moves to the `_park` session, and `F5 s s` brings it back."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s s", "F5 w b / F5 w f", "F5 w q"]
        why: "Panes are found, not arranged, so a new process belongs in the slot you are looking at rather than in a new window. It is a new window in `_park` (the session is made if missing) swapped into this pane with `swap-pane`, and recorded in the pane's history, so `F5 w b` brings the old process back."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 w b / F5 w f"
        title: "Step back and forward through what this pane showed"
        use: "Every process swapped into a pane — by `F5 s s` or `F5 w w` — is remembered for that pane. `F5 w b` brings back the one before, `F5 w f` goes forward again, like back and forward in a browser: swapping something new in after going back drops the forward steps. A process that has since been closed is skipped."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s s", "F5 w w"]
        why: "The history belongs to the place, not the process: the process moves out on every swap, so it is a window option per pane position (`@slot<index>`), and `swap-pane` keeps positions. Going back is itself a swap, so the process you leave goes where the one you bring back was."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 w h"
        title: "Hide this pane"
        use: "Press `F5 w h` and the pane leaves the layout; what runs in it keeps running in the `_park` session. A pane with no name asks first — a popup, `Name it before hiding`: type a name and `Enter`, or `Escape` to keep the pane. The name is the first thing its row shows in `F5 s s`, so type it there and `Enter` brings the pane back into whichever pane you are in."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s s", "F5 w w"]
        why: "Hidden is not closed, and a hidden pane is found again by its name, so the name comes before it goes. The name is the pane option `@name`, not the pane title, because every program in the pane may rewrite its title. It is `break-pane` into `_park` (made if missing); the session's only pane cannot leave, so there a fresh shell stays behind, as `F5 w w`."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 w q"
        title: "Close this pane"
        use: "Press `F5 w q` and the pane you are in closes, with no confirmation."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 w w", "F5 s s"]
        why: "`kill-pane`, next to `w w` so making and closing a pane are one group."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 <arrow>"
        title: "Move mode: walk, split and close panes until Enter"
        use: "Press `F5`, then an arrow: you move one pane that way and stay in move mode. `F5` then Shift or Alt and an arrow splits that way straight away. Arrows keep moving; Alt and an arrow splits greedily — at the top of the layout, so with the window split top/bottom, Alt+→ in the lower pane puts a full-height pane on the right half — and Shift and an arrow splits only the pane you are in (either way the new pane opens in the old one's directory); `q` closes the pane you are in with no confirmation — and you stay in move mode after each. `Enter` or `Escape` ends it; so does any other key, which is dropped rather than typed."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5", "F5 s s"]
        why: "Every move-mode key ends by re-arming the `jump` table, so the mode lasts until you leave it. A key the table lacks falls to the root table and is then dropped, which is what ends the mode on any other key. Alt's split is `split-window -f`, which splits the whole window rather than the pane; Shift's is the plain split. A split inherits the directory through the shell's OSC 7 report (`@cwd`)."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 p r"
        title: "Even the panes out, every level of the layout"
        use: "Press `F5 p r` and the panes in this window are spread evenly — the level you are in, and the levels inside every other pane, so a column you widened by hand stops being wider than its neighbour."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 3
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5", "F5 <arrow>"]
        why: "tmux 3.7c has no `equalize-panes` — it was removed — and the one primitive left, `select-layout -E`, evens the pane you name and the panes directly next to it, so one call straightens one level of the layout tree and not the window. Measured over 50 ragged windows of 2–7 panes as the total sibling size mismatch left behind: once on the window leaves 30.1, iterating that call to a fixed point leaves 9.6 (the active pane's group settles and the groups it never reaches stay ugly), and one window call followed by one call per pane leaves 1.5. That sweep is the whole command, and it is idempotent — pressing it again changes nothing. It UNZOOMS: `-E` cannot lay out a window it cannot see, so a zoomed window comes back unzoomed and evened."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 w z"
        title: "Zen: one pane centred, its neighbours dimmed and cut off beside it"
        use: "Press `F5 w z` and zen mode is on for every window: the active column sits centred at half the screen width, and every other column is the same size — the previous one to its left and the next one to its right, dimmed and cut off by the screen edges, the rest out of sight. A split left or right (Shift or Alt with ←/→) starts a new column; a split up or down stacks a pane inside the column you are in, where ↑/↓ walk the stack. `F5 →` and `F5 ←` cycle through the columns, wrapping at the ends, and leave you in move mode: keep pressing ← or → until the column you want is centred, then Enter; a click on a neighbour or a split brings that column to the centre. The cockpit is half the screen wide and grows down. `F5 w z` again turns it off and tiles the columns side by side. `set -g @zen 1` in tmux.conf starts it on, `@zen-width` changes the half."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 4
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 p r", "F5 <arrow>"]
        why: "tmux has no margins, so the two sides of the centre are blank spacer panes, marked `@zen_spacer` with an empty header; the process chip in the bar and the pane count skip them, and an arrow or click into one lands back on the pane it left. Each pane's column is the pane option `@zen_col`: a new pane in a top/bottom split joins its sibling's column, any other starts its own. The centre is the active column's panes stacked (a `v` cell) at the heights the split or a drag left; every pane of every other column is a floating pane of the frames build, marked `@zen_float`, the column's panes stacked at equal heights within the active column's content size; a floating pane may sit past the window edge and is clipped there, which is what cuts the neighbours off and keeps every column the same size, so nothing reflows as you cycle. In zen an Alt split is the plain pane split, not the whole-window one: `split-window -f` beside the floats fails with `no space for a new pane` on a `separate` border, and the refit places the pane by column anyway. Inactive panes are dimmed by `window-style` as everywhere. The carousel is one `select-layout` of the JSON layout string, which places every pane by index and sets the active one in the same call. The layout, new-window and select-pane hooks refit after every new window, split, close, drag, resize and focus change, and a layout that already fits is left alone — the hook's own call fires it again, so it must be a fixed point. TRAP: `new-window` fires no `window-layout-changed`, so without `after-new-window` a window opened with zen on stayed unfitted until its first split. Zoom is left alone."
    }
    {
        key: "Cmd+← / Cmd+→"
        title: "Step to the previous or next window"
        use: "Cmd+← goes to the previous tmux window, Cmd+→ to the next; both wrap at the ends. The bar's window numbers show where you are."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 5
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["the status bar"]
        why: "WezTerm keeps only local chrome, so it binds nothing itself: it relays Cmd+arrow as Ctrl+Alt+Shift+arrow (`CSI 1;8D` / `CSI 1;8C`), a chord no program here uses, and tmux maps that to `previous-window` / `next-window`."
    }
    {
        cmd: "a lone pane"
        title: "A window with one pane has no border"
        use: "Nothing to press. A window holding one pane shows it with no frame and no header, zen or not; split it and the frames and headers come back."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 6
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 w z"]
        why: "`@lone` counts the window's panes that are not zen spacers or popups inside one tmux format (`#{P:…}`); the layout and new-window hooks set the window's `pane-border-status off` and `pane-border-type joined` when it is one and unset both when it is not. No fork, and each set is guarded by the current value, since the set itself relayouts and fires the hook again."
    }
    {
        cmd: "the status bar"
        title: "Read the bar across the top"
        use: "Nothing to press. The left of the bar is the mode — NORMAL, COCKPIT while `F5` is open, JUMP in move mode, COPY in copy mode, ZEN — then the clock and the machine's load; the right end lists the session's windows by number, the current one in the accent, then a reverse-video chip counting every process (pane) in every session. Over ssh the machine's name appears in each pane's header."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 5
        kind: "concept"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5"]
        why: "Background says which window has focus, foreground says which windows are busy — two signals that never have to be decoded out of one channel. A window counts as busy when any of its panes is running something that is not a shell, read from the pane's foreground process rather than from prompt marking, which is deliberately off. There is no key legend on the bar: the digits are the legend, and a legend in words was tried and removed as noise. The colours are ANSI slots, never fixed values, so a theme switch retints the bar with everything else."
    }
    {
        cmd: "the session survives"
        title: "Close the terminal without losing your work"
        use: "Quit the terminal — or lose the ssh connection — and nothing stops: reopening it puts you back exactly where you were, same windows, same panes, same directories. A reboot costs you at most fifteen minutes of layout changes: the arrangement is saved on a timer and restored when the machine comes back, with editors reopening on the files they had."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 6
        kind: "concept"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["the status bar", "F5"]
        why: "The session outliving the terminal is free — the server is a separate process and the attach is idempotent, so opening the terminal again is the same gesture as opening it the first time. Surviving a REBOOT is not free and is two plugins: one saves and restores the layout, the other runs the timer. What comes back is the shape of the work — where each pane was, what it was running — and not a copy of its scrollback, which would put the output of every command on disk and grow without bound. Editors come back through the editor's own session file rather than by dropping an untracked file in every project directory."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 m"
        title: "Freeze the screen, select text — then modify it"
        use: "Press `F5 m` and the pane stops scrolling: you get a cursor that moves on vi keys — `hjkl` or the arrows to move, `b` back to the start of the word before and `w` forward to the end of the word you are on, `g` and `G` to the ends of the scrollback, `/` to search it. Nothing has to be pressed to begin. Stop where you want and Shift takes a unit outright — `W` the word under the cursor, `L` the rest of the line, `P` the whole paragraph — each copied and out of the mode in one key. Lowercase `p` selects that paragraph and stays, so it can be widened or trimmed before you take it. `Space` grows a selection a press at a time: the link under the cursor, else the word (everything between spaces), then the whole line, then one more line down per press, scrolling along to the end. `V` takes whole lines, and `v` switches between a run of text and a rectangular block. `y`, `Enter` or `Ctrl+C` copy what is selected and leave; `q` or `Esc` leave with nothing copied, and `Esc` with a selection open just drops the selection. With a selection open, `c` arms a transform and the next letter rewrites the selection into a hold buffer instead of the clipboard: `c` lower, `C` upper, `s` snake, `k` kebab, `p` Pascal, `t` title, `a` raw. Any other key disarms and is dropped. Nothing is delivered yet: each transform holds one piece, and pieces can be collected one selection at a time — across panes, the hold survives leaving the mode. `t` at navigation level types the whole hold at the pane's prompt, space-joined, and empties it; `x` empties it untouched. While `c` is held, the status bar lights the transform letters in colour4 — `c` lower, `C` upper, `s` snake, `k` kebab, `p` pascal, `t` title, `a` raw — and shows navigation hints (`SPC` select, `c` transform, `t` type, `x` clear, `y` copy, `q` leave) over the normal copy-mode row, the clock and tmux-sys staying visible; a keypress disarms and is dropped."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "copy"
        step: 1
        does: "enter-copy-mode"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["drag", "Ctrl+C"]
        why: "A function key asks the terminal for nothing, which is why this one replaced a chord. `Ctrl+Shift+X` was the entry until 2026-09-01 and it did not work: the legacy encoding cannot spell it — it is the same byte as a plain `Ctrl+X` — so it only arrives where the terminal has negotiated an extended-key protocol, and where that does not happen the multiplexer sees an unbound key and hands it to the program in the pane. The mode never opened and nothing said why, on a binding documented as working since the cutover. Motions belong to the mode rather than to keys outside it, which is why searching the scrollback is `/` from inside. The selection keys are the multiplexer's own, unchanged: a hand-rolled `c` cycle that grew the selection a step at a time was retired on 2026-09-02, because it needed a hook to reset itself on every exit and a cycle left half-armed for the next time was a real failure. `Space` grows by press instead, and carries no state past the selection: it continues only while a selection is open and the cursor is where its last press left it, so any motion (which clears the selection) or leaving the mode starts it over — nothing to reset on exit. The one motion that is not a default is `w`, and it is the word END: stock `w` is the start of the NEXT word and it wraps onto the line below, so it takes you off the line you were reading; the end-of-word motion never leaves its line and parks at the last word end. The Shift row and `p` are the selection keys the retired cycle was reaching for, one unit each instead of one key cycling through them, and they need no `Space` first. Shift is the whole rule: one unit, copied and gone in one key. Only the paragraph has a select-only twin, because `w` and `l` are motions and stay motions — `l` was tried as the line-end selection and handed back, since shadowing the vi cursor-right to save a Shift is not a trade worth making. `l` shadows the vi cursor-right, and that is the trade it was worth: the arrows move the cursor and always have, so `hjk` keeps three of the four and the fourth buys a selection key under the fingers. `P` and `p` are the pair with a constraint behind them: the multiplexer has no select-paragraph command, and the obvious build — grow whatever is selected by a paragraph — mis-anchors, so it takes the whole paragraph the cursor is in every time. Reading it as widening is what makes that right rather than a limitation. A cursor motion is not a way to widen a word selection: it leaves copy mode in the multiplexer's own word-selection state, where a motion grows by whole words and can reach backwards past the word taken."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 m s"
        title: "Resume the Claude session this pane already printed"
        use: "In copy mode, reads back through this pane's scrollback for the newest `--resume` or `--continue` id, leaves the mode, and types `cr <id>` at the prompt WITHOUT running it — press Enter at the prompt, or edit the line first. Press the gesture again and it steps one id older, wrapping round to the newest; the status line says which of how many you are on. A pane that has never printed a session id says so and types nothing."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "agents"
        step: 2
        does: "resume-claude"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["cr [...args]", "F5 m", "cc [...args]"]
        why: "`cr` on its own offers every session recorded for the directory, and they are uuids: the list cannot tell you which one is the session that just died in front of you. The scrollback can, because that session printed its own id there. So the pick is made from what this pane has seen rather than from what the directory holds, and the two routes stay side by side. The multiplexer can find the text without help — copy mode's search takes an extended regex — but it cannot KEEP the part that matched: a format hands over the whole cursor line and has no capture group, which is the one reason there is a script behind this key at all. Copy mode is left before anything is typed, because a pane sitting in a mode swallows sent keys and there is no route from inside the mode to the shell. The line is typed and not run: which session to resume is a decision, and the gesture stops one key short of making it. The cycle keeps its place in two pane options and resets when the NEWEST id in the pane changes — that is, exactly when a new session has announced itself. There is no timer and no hook to leave half-armed, which is what the widening `c` cycle retired on 2026-09-02 could not manage. It stays `s`: `c` is the transform arm since 2026-10-05 — the freed key was taken back for `c c`…, because a hand reaching for `c` in this mode is reaching for the rewrite, and `s` answers before the arm can eat it."
    }
    {
        key: "drag"
        title: "Select with the mouse and have it on the clipboard"
        use: "Drag across text with the left button and let go — the selection is on the system clipboard, ready for `Cmd+V` anywhere. Double-click copies the word under the pointer, triple-click the whole line. A drag stays inside the pane it started in."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "copy"
        step: 2
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+C", "Ctrl+V", "F5 m"]
        why: "The selection belongs to the multiplexer, not the terminal, and it has to: a terminal-level drag across a vertical split runs straight through the divider and takes both panes\' columns as one line. Having taken the gesture the multiplexer owes it a clipboard, and that is the part that shipped broken — every stock copy path, the drag-release and both click counts included, pipes to NO command, which fills the multiplexer\'s own paste buffer and stops. The text highlighted, it looked copied, and `Cmd+V` still pasted whatever was there before. Each path now names the sink explicitly. A program that tracks the mouse itself — the editor, an agent pane — still gets its own clicks."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl+V"
        title: "Paste the clipboard into whatever is running"
        use: "Press `Ctrl+V` in any pane — a shell prompt, nvim, a running agent alike — and the clipboard lands in one piece: nothing to confirm, and no reindenting in the editor. On a machine reached over ssh this key is not there; use whatever your own terminal pastes with, and it arrives the same way."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "copy"
        step: 5
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+C"]
        why: "Bracketed paste is the mechanism: the text arrives wrapped in the escape sequence that marks it as pasted rather than typed, so a program that would reindent or autocomplete every keystroke can tell the two apart. That marker is also the only route clipboard-based dictation has into a terminal. This is the one gesture on this page that is deliberately NOT the multiplexer's, and it is the exception `07-multiplexer` I1 names: only the program sitting in front of the human can read that human's clipboard, so pasting belongs to the terminal by construction and cannot be made portable. Copying can — which is why every copy path here is the multiplexer's and this one is not."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl+C"
        title: "Copy the selection, or interrupt when there is none"
        use: "In copy mode with something selected, `Ctrl+C` copies it and leaves; with nothing selected it just leaves. Outside copy mode it is the ordinary interrupt and reaches the program as SIGINT, so you never spend a stop on a copy that was not there."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "copy"
        step: 3
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+V", "F5 m", "drag"]
        why: "Two bindings in series, and the order is the whole trick. The terminal's reads ITS own selection first and sends the key on when there is none — which under the multiplexer is always, since the multiplexer owns the mouse and the terminal never sees the drag. So the key always arrives, and the multiplexer decides: copy when a selection is present, leave when it is not. Corrected 2026-09-01. This said mouse selection was left to the terminal because the multiplexer's mouse mode was off; the mouse mode is ON and has been, so the terminal's half found nothing to copy and the multiplexer's half was bound to plain `cancel` — the obvious gesture, select and press `Ctrl+C`, threw the selection away and touched no clipboard."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl+Alt+Super+drag"
        title: "Move the window without a titlebar"
        use: "Hold all three of `Ctrl`, `Alt` and `Cmd`, then drag anywhere in the window with the left button and the whole OS window follows. An ordinary drag still selects text."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 9
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+click"]
        why: "`RESIZE` decorations leave no titlebar to grab, so this is the only handle for repositioning the window. The deliberately heavy three-modifier combo is what keeps it from stealing plain clicks and selection drags."
    }
    {
        key: "Alt+Enter"
        title: "Leave or re-enter fullscreen"
        use: "Press `Alt+Enter` (Option+Enter) and the WezTerm window leaves fullscreen and stays a normal window through resizes and display changes. Press it again and it fills the screen again."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 11
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+Alt+Super+drag"]
        why: "Every window is kept fullscreen by a resize handler that re-fills any window not filling its screen, so a plain toggle would be undone at once. The key marks the window it takes out of fullscreen, and the handler leaves a marked window alone. WezTerm's own default for this key is switched off with the rest of its defaults, which is why it is bound here."
    }
    {
        key: "Shift+click"
        title: "Open the link under the cursor"
        use: "Hold `Shift` and left-click a URL or a hyperlinked file path — in a plain shell, tmux, Claude or nvim — and it opens in the browser or editor. A plain click still reaches the program."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 10
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["Ctrl+Alt+Super+drag"]
        why: "Three measured facts: `mouse_reporting` on the duplicate binding is what keeps it working while an application captures the mouse; tmux must also forward the underlying OSC 8 (`terminal-features ,*:hyperlinks`), a feature only a client attached after it was set receives; and the click's Down stroke is Nopped, because with only Up bound tmux takes the press and turns the click into a copy-mode selection."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 s c"
        title: "Pick a channel, then a row, and act on it"
        use: "Press `F5 s c` in any pane and a list of channels floats over it in a centred box, already drawn when it appears. Type until one is left and it opens by itself (or press `Enter`), then pick a row: a file or a text match opens in the editor below your pane, a directory opens a shell there, a commit is shown, a history line lands on a fresh prompt unrun. The `theme` and `font` channels preview live over the whole terminal and `Enter` keeps the pick. Any other channel types its row into your pane."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 3
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s t", "F5 s q", "F5 t s"]
        why: "F5 s q is the same two steps with the query typed first. The font channel lives only here: the font is WezTerm's, so a pick is written to `~/.local/state/wezterm/font.txt`, which the WezTerm config watches, and the live preview is a user variable WezTerm turns into a per-window override. The channel step and the theme and font pickers run in fzf, not television: television 0.15.9 only auto-accepts a single entry at startup and caches each row's preview, so going back to a row would not repaint. fzf's `one:accept` and its per-focus preview do both."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl-x in the files picker"
        title: "Copy, move or open the picked file"
        use: "In the files channel, `Ctrl-x` lists every action by name. `Ctrl-b` copies the file's contents to the clipboard. `copy_file` copies the file itself, so it pastes into Finder or Mail. `Ctrl-g` moves it: a second picker asks for the directory (`Enter` picks it), then a prompt asks for the name, and `Enter` on an empty name keeps the old one. `F12` still opens the file in the editor."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 3
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s c", "F5 s q"]
        why: "They are television's own channel actions (`[actions.*]` in `files.toml`), so they work wherever the files channel opens. Every picker has the same shape: the preview on top, the results under it, the input line at the bottom, like telescope's vertical layout (`orientation = portrait` in television's `config.toml`). An action ends the picker and prints nothing, so `F5 s c` has no row left to act on afterwards. The move asks `mv -i` before overwriting anything."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl-. in the pickers"
        title: "Cycle the search modes: normal, hidden files, gitignored files"
        use: "In every picker that searches files or folders — the files, dirs, text and recent-files channels of television, plus telescope find_files and live_grep in the editor — `ctrl-.` cycles the mode: normal, then hidden files, then gitignored files, then back. Television names the mode in the border; telescope names it in the prompt title. The shipped `ctrl-s` cycles the same sources in television."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 4
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s c", "finder", "F5 s q"]
        why: "fd, rg and telescope skip hidden and gitignored paths by default, and the files a search most needs — a dotfile, an untracked build output — are often exactly those. Television cycles its channel sources natively (`config.toml` binds `ctrl-.`), but the key only reaches a pane that speaks csi-u: television never asks tmux for it, so the nushell tv def and tv-go enable it around the picker, the pre_prompt hook in config.nu restores a pane a killed run leaves enabled, and modal turns extended-keys on for its popup server. Telescope reopens the picker with the same query — hidden or no_ignore for files, --hidden or --no-ignore for grep — and one mode index is shared, so the editor keeps one visibility state."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 s t"
        title: "Pick a channel, then a row, and type it into this pane"
        use: "Same two steps as `F5 s c`, but whatever you pick is typed into the pane you pressed `F5 s t` in, unrun — a path, a commit hash, a branch, a history line."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 4
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s c"]
    }
    {
        key: "F5 t s"
        title: "Pick a colour scheme from anywhere"
        use: "Press `F5 t s` in any pane — a shell, an editor, an agent — and the scheme picker floats over it in a centred box. Moving through the list retints the whole terminal live; `Enter` keeps the pick, `Esc` puts back what you had. The list shows only schemes of the mode you are in — dark or light — and the pick becomes that mode's scheme; the other mode, which `F5 t t` switches to, stays as it was."
        topic: "config"
        task: "appearance"
        step: 3
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 t t", "theme"]
        why: "It is `F5 s c` with the channel step skipped, so it works over a full-screen program. F5 t t is a dark/light switch, so the picker is filtered to the active mode and a pick can only ever replace that mode's scheme."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 s q"
        title: "Search: say what, then pick the channel"
        use: "Press `F5 s q` in any pane — a shell, an editor, a running agent — and a one-line box titled `search` opens. Type what you are looking for and press enter; the channel list opens, and whichever channel you pick opens with your query already typed into its row list. Pick a row and it acts exactly as `F5 s c` does: files and matches open in the editor below your pane, a directory opens a shell there, anything else lands on a fresh prompt unrun. Esc at any step leaves nothing behind. To search your whole machine, `cd ~` first."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 1
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 s c", "finder", "Ctrl-Q"]
        why: "It is `F5 s c` with the query first — one script (`tv-go find`), and the channels under `~/.config/television/cable` are the only list of places to search. It replaced `tv-all`, a 456-line second copy of those channels that counted hits per channel and ran a background scan of `$HOME`; the counts cost a hand-written lane per channel, and `$HOME` needed a cache, a second ignore list and a reaper. It is a tmux binding rather than a reedline one so that it fires inside nvim and inside an agent pane; the query comes from tmux's own `command-prompt`, whose Escape cancels silently. A bare tmux popup is painted as an empty box before television draws, a visible flash, so `modal` starts the picker in a hidden session first and opens the popup only once the picker has drawn."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 t w"
        title: "Pick a wallpaper, darkness, blur and tint"
        use: "Press `F5 t w` in any pane and the images in `~/wallpapers` float over it. The row you stop on is drawn behind the terminal at once; `←`/`→` darken or lighten it in steps of 5, `Shift+←`/`Shift+→` blur or sharpen it in steps of 4 px, `Alt+←`/`Alt+→` lay the theme's background colour over it in steps of 10 % (0 shows only the image, 100 only the colour), and the preview pane shows all three values. `Enter` keeps the pick, `Esc` puts back the image you had, and the `off` row removes the image altogether."
        topic: "config"
        task: "appearance"
        step: 4
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["F5 t s", "F5 t f"]
        why: "Nothing in a tmux popup can draw a picture — the picker runs in a nested tmux server, which eats graphics escapes — so the preview is the wallpaper itself: every focus runs `wezterm-bg` on the row and WezTerm's reload watch repaints. Darkness is applied by WezTerm and costs nothing — on a curve where every 20 halves the brightness, so the image nears black without ever reaching it and the dark end stays fine-tunable; blur is baked in by magick, which is why it moves in coarser steps."
    }
    {
        key: "F5 t t"
        title: "Switch the colour scheme from anywhere"
        use: "Press `F5 t t` in any pane — even over a full-screen TUI — and the scheme parked in the other slot is applied at once: the terminal's own colours, the status bar, and every pane. Press it again to come back. It works on any terminal that honours the standard colour escapes, including one at the far end of an ssh."
        topic: "config"
        task: "appearance"
        step: 2
        does: "switch-scheme"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["theme toggle", "F5"]
        why: "Bound in the multiplexer rather than the shell, because a full-screen TUI would swallow a shell-level binding; run in the background, because the theme tool's hook chain would otherwise block the server for its duration. The scheme reaches the terminal as escape sequences written straight to it, not through a file only one emulator could read — which is why the key is worth pressing on a machine you ssh'd into. It is the one key that is never forwarded to a nested session: the palette belongs to the outermost terminal."
    }

    # -------------------------------------------------------------- containers
    {
        key: "Ctrl+Shift+D"
        title: "Drop this directory into a capsule"
        use: "Press `Ctrl+Shift+D` in any pane: `capsule` runs on that pane's directory and you end up at a shell in `/workspace` inside the container, with the directory's contents there and your credentials working. Press it again later and you are back in the same running container, immediately."
        topic: "containers"
        task: "containers"
        step: 1
        does: "enter-capsule"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["capsule [dir]", "Ctrl+Shift+B"]
        why: "The binding is a thin wrapper over the CLI, not a second implementation — same container name, same image, same mounts, which is checkable by diffing `docker inspect`. The old config had two entry paths that drifted apart, and that is the mistake being avoided. It is one of the four keys the terminal still owns after the multiplexer took the rest: it types into the pane rather than addressing it."
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl+Shift+B"
        title: "Rebuild the capsule image and start over"
        use: "Press `Ctrl+Shift+B` to run `capsule --rebuild` for this directory: the image is built again and the container recreated. Reach for it when you changed the image on purpose, or when a container has gone strange."
        topic: "containers"
        task: "containers"
        step: 2
        does: "rebuild-capsule"
        mode: "terminal"
        also: ["capsule --rebuild", "Ctrl+Shift+D"]
        why: "The key looked contested — in the live config control+shift+B prompts for a desktop wallpaper — but that whole pipeline is rated DO NOT PORT, so it comes free with the port and there is nothing left to settle. Why a rebuild is ever needed at all lives on `capsule --rebuild`, not here."
    }
]
